# Chapter 11 of the Reference Manual: the messages of all the programs.
# The chapter is kept in five files that this one includes.
.doc Strata Reference: Messages
.chapter 11
.h1 Messages [messages]
This chapter lists every message that the programs of Strata show or write:
the lines on the message line of the Editor, the boxes, the questions, the
notices of the Document Manager and the lines that the Printer and the
print and preview jobs write.  Each message has its text exactly as the program shows it, what
it means, and what to do about it.  The messages are grouped by program and by
the area that shows them, and in every table they are sorted by their text,
ignoring capital letters, so that a message is found by the first words that
the screen showed.  The index at the end of the manual lists every message
too.  What the programs do not show on a screen is written to the log of the
run ({n:appcfg-log}).
.ix Appendix, error messages
.ix Error messages
.ix Messages
.ix Warnings
.ix Notices
.ix Message index
.h2 How to read this chapter [msg-reading]
.ix Messages, notation
.ix Capitals in messages
.ix Message text, changing parts
.ix Program names in messages
A message is written in the program font.  Where the program puts a value into
the text, the table shows a word in capital letters in its place:
.table 9 51
Word | Stands for
NAME | A file name or a name of any other kind (a library entry, a macro key,
 | a printer queue).
PATH | A full file name, with its directories. A path that was typed without a
 | directory is made full by the program that shows it.
DIR | The name of a directory.
FILE | A file name, as above.
N | A number.  In the messages with a word that changes between the singular
 | and the plural, as in {c:1 replacement} and {c:3 replacements}, the table
 | shows the plural.
REASON | The reason that the operating system gives, in its own words: for
 | example {c:No such file or directory}, {c:Permission denied},
 | {c:No space left on device}.  The words depend on the system and its language settings.
TEXT | Words that come from another program (LaTeX, the print system) or from
 | the document, copied unchanged.
LABEL | The label of a field of a panel, as the panel shows it.
.end
.note Some messages contain the name of a program of Strata.  They are shown
  here with the name of the program replaced by a word in angle brackets:
  {c:<Editor>}, {c:<Document Manager>}, {c:<Printer>} or {c:<Viewer>}.  The
  screen shows the real name of the program in that place.
Messages that other programs write, such as the first error line of LaTeX or
the answer of the print command, are shown unchanged inside a Strata message;
{x:msg-other} says what the commonest of them mean.  Where another chapter
already describes a message with its context, the entry here has a reference
to that section: this chapter is the one place where all the messages can be
looked up, and the other chapters tell what the command was doing.
.h2 Where messages appear [msg-where]
.ix Message line
.ix Boxes
.ix Footer, Document Manager
.ix Standard error
.ix Press any key
Strata shows a message in one of five ways.  The tables of this chapter say
which way each message uses.
.table 19 41
Way | What it looks like
Message line (Editor) | One line in bold reverse video at the bottom of the
 | screen, over the second row of key labels.  No key has to be pressed; the
 | line goes away at the next key ({n:view-message}).
Box with Press any key | A framed box in the middle of the screen with a title
 | (the name of the command or of the area), the text, and the words
 | {c:Press any key}.  The key that closes it is lost: it is not used as a command.  The command
 | that showed the box is over, or its panel is shown again; each table says
 | which.
Question | A framed box or a line with the choices Yes and No. {k:Esc} counts
 | as No.
Footer (Document Manager) | The line at the bottom of the Document Manager,
 | which shows hints such as {c:F1 Help Esc Cancel}, and the text of a message
 | in their place until the next key.
Error output | Text written on the standard error of the shell that started
 | the program, in the terminal, outside the screen.  It is seen when the
 | program was started from a shell, or in the box that the Document Manager
 | makes of it.
.end
The messages of the print and preview jobs are made once and shown in the way
of the command that ran the job: on the message line, in a box, or on the
footer of the Document Manager.  They are therefore in one section ({n:msg-job}).
.h2 The sections of this chapter [msg-sections]
.ix Messages, list of sections
.table 22 38
Section | Contents
{x:msg-editor-line} | The message line of the Editor.
{x:msg-editor-boxes} | The boxes of the Editor, by area: File, Format, Marker,
 | Edit, Print and Preview, Tools (Macros, Library, Spell, Thesaurus), the
 | equation and the start of the Editor.
{x:msg-editor-questions} | The questions of the Editor.
{x:msg-job} | The messages of a print and of a preview (Print panel and
 | Preview).
{x:msg-printer} | The Printer: the messages that the Editor shows for it.
{x:msg-docmgr} | The Document Manager: footer, boxes, notices and the error
 | output.
{x:msg-spell} | Spell check and thesaurus.
{x:msg-eq} | Equations.
{x:msg-start} | What the programs write at start: no terminal, files that
 | cannot be read.
{x:msg-other} | Messages of programs that Strata runs: LaTeX, the print
 | command, the spell checker, the font installer.
{x:msg-faults} | Messages that look wrong: what they report.
{x:msg-errlog} | The faults that Print and Preview name, and the messages of
 | the Error Log, of the Print Error markers and of Find Next Error.
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: {x:msg-cmdline} has the exit status of the programs and the
messages of their command lines.
.endif
